<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1531–<a href="/years/1614">1614</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Black Tom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond, known as Black Tom, was a powerful Irish nobleman raised at the English court and a cousin and favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. He fought his rivals the FitzGeralds of Desmond and commanded the forces that crushed the second Desmond Rebellion. He built the Elizabethan manor house at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrick-on-suir">Carrick-on-Suir</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-butler-10th-earl-of-ormond.jpg" alt="Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steven van der Meulen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steven_van_der_Meulen_Thomas_Butler_Earl_of_Ormonde_NGI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Butler was born about February 1531 in southern Ireland, the eldest son of James Butler, later 9th Earl of Ormond, and Joan FitzGerald, daughter of James FitzGerald, 10th Earl of Desmond. His father's family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butler-dynasty">Butler dynasty</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-walter">Theobald Walter</a>, appointed Chief Butler of Ireland by King Henry II in 1177. In May 1544, aged about 13, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to be brought up at court, where he took on English speech, dress and manners and the Protestant religion. There he met the young Elizabeth, his distant cousin through her mother Anne Boleyn; she later called him her black husband.</p><p>His father died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 28 October 1546 after being poisoned at a banquet at Ely House, probably at the instigation of the Lord Deputy Anthony St Leger, and Thomas succeeded as 10th Earl of Ormond and 3rd Earl of Ossory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Knighted at Edward VI's coronation in 1547, he fought at the Battle of Pinkie that September and helped put down Wyatt's rebellion in 1554. Elizabeth made him Lord Treasurer of Ireland on 26 August 1559, an office he held for life. His long feud with the neighbouring Earls of Desmond, both families more or less Gaelicized Old English and much intermarried, led to the private Battle of Affane on 8 February 1565, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-fitzgerald-14th-earl-of-desmond">Gerald FitzGerald, 14th Earl of Desmond</a>, was shot in the hip by Ormond's brother Edmund and captured.</p><p>In the first Desmond Rebellion some of his own brothers joined the rebels, and three were attainted in 1570. When the second rising began in 1579 he was made governor of Munster and led the royal army that suppressed it, moved, it is held, as much by rivalry with the Desmonds as by religion. Both rebellions left Munster desolate for many years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was made a Knight of the Garter in 1588 and carried the sword of state before the Queen at Tilbury. In 1600, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Tyrone's Rebellion</a>, he was held captive from April to June by Owny MacRory O'More. In the 1560s he had built the Tudor manor house at Ormonde Castle, meant to receive the Queen, who planned visits in 1602 and 1603 that never took place. He married three times; his sons by Elizabeth Sheffield died young, and he died at Carrick on 22 November 1614 and was buried in St Canice's Cathedral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>. The earldom passed to his nephew Walter of the Kilcash branch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German agents set off explosions at the <b>Black Tom</b> depot in New York Harbor, destroying munitions bound for the Allies.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Butler</b>, the 10th Earl, helped crush the Desmond rebellions; James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde, was Lord Lieutenant under Charles II.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ormond">Earldom of Ormond</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrick-on-suir">Carrick-on-Suir</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-7.41&amp;year=1584" aria-label="Carrick-on-Suir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4614 5631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4614 5631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-7.41&amp;year=1584">What was here in 1584? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
